WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.237
When you connect software to a video API, the visual output is only part of what

2
00:00:06.237 --> 00:00:06.861
matters.

3
00:00:06.861 --> 00:00:11.297
A generative video API creates new footage from instructions.

4
00:00:11.297 --> 00:00:17.194
A rendering API composes your supplied assets and scene rules into finished files.

5
00:00:17.194 --> 00:00:21.896
Some workflows need both, plus voice and approval steps.

6
00:00:21.896 --> 00:00:27.827
Before integrating either, define your input and output contract: what your system

7
00:00:27.827 --> 00:00:33.541
supplies, what identifiers come back, and how long downloads remain accessible.

8
00:00:33.541 --> 00:00:36.165
Design around asynchronous jobs.

9
00:00:36.165 --> 00:00:42.174
Submitting a render and completing one are separate events, so retain a job record

10
00:00:42.174 --> 00:00:46.498
with its identifier and current status after every request.

11
00:00:46.498 --> 00:00:52.599
Use an application-level key to prevent duplicate submissions when a network request

12
00:00:52.599 --> 00:00:53.325
times out.

13
00:00:53.325 --> 00:00:59.122
Verify callbacks using the provider's documented method, and tolerate duplicate

14
00:00:59.122 --> 00:01:03.158
notifications without moving a completed job backwards.

15
00:01:03.158 --> 00:01:08.696
Test invalid inputs, timeouts, and repeated notifications before scaling.

16
00:01:08.696 --> 00:01:14.661
Measure time to approved output, failures, and total cost at the pilot stage.
